# Ticket starter

The `.pdlc/bin/spine.js start-ticket` command is deterministic
orchestration — route the ticket to a track, create a git worktree on
its own branch, initialize phase tracking there. It exists specifically
so an agent can run it via Bash, not so a human types it. **If you find
yourself telling the user to run a `spine` command themselves, stop —
that's this skill's job.**

## When to use this

The user says something like "let's start BANK-1234," "pick up this
bug," "I've been assigned three tickets, start the first one" — any
expression of intent to begin work on a specific ticket, whether or
not they mention this framework by name.

## What to do

1. **Get the ticket's fields** — `issue_type`, `priority`, `labels`.
   If a Jira MCP or similar integration is connected in this session,
   use it to fetch them. Otherwise ask the user directly ("what's the
   priority and issue type on BANK-1234?") — don't guess plausible
   values; a wrong guess routes to the wrong track silently.

2. **Run the command via Bash**, from the repo root (or point `--repo`
   at it):
   ```bash
   node .pdlc/bin/spine.js start-ticket \
     --id BANK-1234 --issue-type Bug --priority P2
   ```
   `--labels` is optional — omit it when the ticket has none. Pass it as
   a comma-separated list when it does: `--labels hotfix,needs-design`.

   If `.pdlc/` doesn't exist in this repo, this client hasn't had the
   runtime distributed yet — that's a setup gap to flag, not something
   to work around by improvising the routing/worktree logic yourself.
   The whole point of a deterministic command is that it behaves
   identically every time; reimplementing its logic ad hoc defeats
   that.

   The command also refuses to run if `.pdlc/` exists but isn't
   committed — a worktree only ever contains committed files, so an
   uncommitted runtime would produce a worktree with no engine in it.
   If that's the error, the fix is to commit `.pdlc/` (along with
   `.claude/`, `.cursor/` and `.github/workflows/pdlc-gate-check.yml`),
   not to pass a flag past it.

   If the branch already exists — developer B picking up a ticket
   developer A started — the command re-attaches a worktree to that
   existing branch rather than failing. That's the intended path for
   handovers; you don't need to invent a new branch name.

3. **Translate the JSON result into plain language**, don't dump it
   raw. The output includes `ticketDir` (the absolute path to
   `<worktree>/pdlc/BANK-1234`), `worktreePath`, `branch`, `track` and
   `startingPhase`:
   > "Started BANK-1234 — routed to the emergency track (P2 bug), set
   > up a worktree at `../payments-api-BANK-1234` on branch
   > `pdlc/BANK-1234`, starting at the develop phase since emergency
   > skips straight there. This ticket's artifacts go in
   > `pdlc/BANK-1234/` inside that worktree."

   Right after, run `node .pdlc/bin/spine.js spec-kitti --ticket
   BANK-1234` (add `--epic <KEY>` if you fetched the parent epic). If the
   repo uses spec-kitti (the client's Spec Kit fork) and a feature
   already covers this ticket, say so in the same breath — "this story
   is already specified in `specs/007-exportar-extractos/`, the writers
   will extract from there" — so nobody re-derives what exists. If it
   reports no spec-kitti feature, say nothing about it; that's the
   normal case.

   Then, if the repo has `pdlc/jira-status-map.yaml`, run the
   `jira-sync` discipline so the Jira board shows the starting phase
   right away; without that file it is a no-op, and its failure never
   blocks the ticket.

4. **Tell the user to open the worktree as their workspace, and say it
   explicitly.** This is the step people miss. The worktree is at
   `../<repo>-<TICKET>` — a sibling directory, *outside* the folder
   their editor currently has open, so nothing in their current window
   will show the ticket's files:
   - **Cursor:** File > Open Folder, and pick the `worktreePath` from
     the output.
   - **Claude Code:** start a new `claude` session inside the worktree,
     or run `claude --add-dir <worktreePath>` to bring it into the
     current session.

   Then work there for the rest of the ticket. Every gating artifact
   for it — `intent.md`, `scope.md`, `spec.md`, `plan.md`,
   `release.md` — plus `phase-state.json` and `memory/runs/` lives in
   `pdlc/<TICKET-ID>/` inside that worktree, committed on the ticket
   branch together with the code, and merged to main with the PR. Not
   the repo root: root-level artifacts made every later ticket inherit
   the previous ticket's approvals, which is a governance bypass, and
   made two concurrent tickets conflict on the same files.

5. **If the user has multiple tickets to start**, run this once per
   ticket — each gets its own worktree. Don't try to work multiple
   tickets in one shared working directory; that's exactly what
   `pdlc/<TICKET-ID>/phase-state.json` being per-ticket is designed to
   prevent colliding.

## What this explicitly does not do

- Doesn't decide the track itself — routing is `.pdlc/phase-graph.yaml`'s
  `routing.rules`, evaluated deterministically by the command. This
  skill's only judgment call is gathering accurate inputs.

  Those rules match this client's own scheme (`'P1'`, `'Bug'`). Jira
  Cloud's default priority names are `Highest`/`High`/`Medium`/`Low`,
  and some projects use lowercase issue types. If what you fetched
  doesn't look like the rule values, don't quietly translate it and
  don't invent a mapping — say so, because every unmatched ticket falls
  through to the default `standard` track, silently.
- Doesn't draft `intent.md` — that's `jira-intake` (or whichever
  module fills `plan.intake`). This skill only gets the ticket to the
  point where that work can start.
- Doesn't approve anything — approval is always a human step, and here
  it takes three fields in the relevant artifact's frontmatter:
  `status: approved`, `approved_by: <name>`, and
  `approval_evidence: <link>` to where the human actually approved
  (Jira comment, Slack message, email). `require_approval_evidence` is
  on for this client, so the gate blocks without the link — including
  when the approval was said straight to you in session. See
  `phase-advancer`.
- Doesn't clean up the worktree once the ticket's done — that's
  `.pdlc/bin/spine.js finish-ticket`, the counterpart to this command.
  Run it (also via Bash, same discipline) once the user confirms a
  ticket is actually finished, not left for worktrees to accumulate
  forever. It refuses to remove one that's still in progress unless
  `--force true` is passed explicitly (abandoning the ticket).
